all of the spider-man films tend to  feature the same elements over and over  again well a lot of its worked well for  the character for about sixty years you  could go to the theater and play Spidey  bingo pretty easily here are things that  we see in every single spider-man movie  let's start with a gimme here if you  have a nephew or niece and you find out  they've been bitten by a radioactive  spider and developed fantastic abilities  like sticking to walls or super-strength  pack your bags and get out of town as  fast as you can otherwise you're headed  for disaster admittedly the death of his  uncle is the most important part of  spider-man's origin story but still  watching an old man get shot starts to  lose its impact when you've seen it over  and over which is especially problematic  in the Sam Raimi spider-man trilogy we  get Uncle Ben's murder happening in the  first film a flashback and tons of  references to it and spider-man 2 and a  completely different version of it that  involves the sad sack movie version of  The Sandman making up a huge chunk of  the plots of spider-man 3 remember Ben  Parker the old man is shot down in cold  blood it should probably be noted that  this particular brand of tragedy isn't  limited to Ben in spider-man into the  spider verse Miles Morales watches as  his own uncle is killed in front of him  as for the MCU those movies have managed  to avoid even a mention of the late Ben  Parker so far with only a couple of  oblique references to Peters defining  tragedy far from home however is going  to take place in the aftermath of Tony  Stark's death he's not Peters uncle by  relation but he's definitely served as a  father figure and if there was ever  someone in Peters life who fit the weird  uncle archetype it's that guy one of the  most interesting choices in spider-man's  design is that he's one of the few  American superheroes whose costume  leaves him completely covered from head  to toe his mask doesn't even have a  mouth just a pair of big opaque eyes  this works pretty well in comics but in  movies where actors use their faces to  you know act it presents a bit of a  problem that's probably why the  spider-man we see in the movie theater  seems like he'll do whatever he can to  get out of that mask regardless of how  it impacts his secret identity again the  Raimi trilogy is the biggest offender in  this regard  not only does Peter's mass gets shredded  by billions with a frequency that makes  you wonder  just how many spares he has laying  around he'll occasionally just whip the  whole thing off  sure it's a lot easier to tell a  character struggling when you can see  their face but eventually people are  going to wonder why that kid who's  always taking pictures of spider-man  never seems to catch him without his  mask on along the same lines Home Coming  features a recreation of the famous  comic book scene where Spidey fights his  way out from underneath tons of rubble  in the comic the mask stays on but on  the big screen Tom Holland Spidey takes  his mask off to get some fresh air and  keeps it off so that we can see  empowering through his super heroic feat  of strength Andrew Garfield's Peter  Parker shows off his face an amazing  spider-man to help calm down a boy who's  in danger it even extends the animated  acting in into the spider verse where  Spidey loses his mask just before he's  killed by the kingpin and Peter bee  spends most of the movie unmasked and in  sweatpants there are very few things  that superhero movies love as much as a  good old-fashioned dose of crucifixion  imagery you might recall the scene in  Man of Steel for instance where our  hero's ghostly space father tells the  son he sent to earth that he can save  everyone prompting Superman to stick his  arms out and start hovering in a pose  that's pretty familiar to anyone who's  ever been inside a church well that's  the least subtle example of this trope  spider-man's movies have even more of it  in action that's probably to be expected  in a story of great power and great  responsibility but once you start  noticing it you'll find it happens a lot  Spidey's stopping the train in  spider-man 2 is the most obvious  instance but when Peter tries to hold  the ferry together in homecoming we get  the same thing in a pretty similar shot  spider-man 3 even has an entire sequence  that takes place in a church finally  adding parasitic alien symbiotes the  classic biblical imagery into the spider  verse actually manages to parody the  movies crucifix ation twice in the first  two minutes including a shot showing a  Spidey bathed and rays of sunlight that  give him just a tiny bit of a halo one  of the most compelling parts of  spider-man's story is the impact that  his super heroic career has on his soap  opera of a love life it's the source of  endless drama and it usually gets pretty  complicated the same goes for the movies  every single spider-man film has a love  story right there at the core of its  plot with all the twists and turns  that you'd expect in the rainy films it  was Peters on-again off-again  relationship with Mary Jane Watson which  started out complicated and only gets  more tangled as the films continue  especially when Gwen Stacy shows up in  spider-man 3 an amazing spider-man Gwen  was free of a love triangle but that  relationship had no shortage of speed  bumps in the first film Gwen's dad  wanted to arrest spider-man for his  vigilante activity I think he stands for  what you stand for so protecting  innocent people from bad guys I stand  for law and order son that's what I  stand for okay I wear a badge  this guy wears a mask and in the second  a series of spats wound up leading to  Gwen's tragic death  homecoming through a different kind of  wrench into the gears with Peters crush  on classmate Liz not only did he have to  deal with constantly letting his friends  down due to his arachnid activities he  also had to contend with the reveal that  her dad was a supervillain who figured  out Peter was spider-man literally ten  minutes after meeting him good old  spider-man into the spider verse  complicates matters further thanks to  its dimension-hopping plots we've got a  Peter Parker who's dealing with a  divorce from Mary Jane with whom he's  clearly still in love and a different  Mary Jane who's dealing with the death  of her worlds Peter Parker and those are  just the minor characters the main  romantic tension in that movie is  between two people from different  realities that's a tough one there is  long-distance relationships and then  there's my girlfriend you don't know her  she lives on earth 65 spider-man's  roster of enemies is packed with  characters who are actually pretty  sympathetic unlike same murderous  clown's I'd only want to watch the world  burn super villains like the lizard and  the Sandman have motivations that we can  relate to and come with a heaping  helping of pathos that the movies are  more than willing to bring to the screen  the Green Goblin and spider-man is the  outlier here since it's pretty hard to  be sympathetic when your pumpkin bombing  an old lady's bedroom while she's saying  her bedtime prayers  that again even he has a scene where  Norman Osborn is being tormented by his  own alternate personality Doc Ock and  spider-man 2 is shown to be a pretty  decent guy before he's driven to  multi-armed criminal insanity and an  amazing spider-man Curt Connors the  lizard just wants to regrow his lost arm  the vulture in homecoming is maybe the  least sympathetic guy of the bunch not  kill you and everybody loved for all his  talk about sticking up for the little  guy Adrian Toomes drives a Jaguar and  owns a massive house in New York City  bought and paid for with the profits  from his high-tech arms-dealing business  but still his stated motivation of  wanting to take care of his family is  hard to argue with and if nothing else  he keeps Peters identity a secret rather  than seeking revenge into the spider  verse goes the furthest with this not  only do we see the Prowler as a regular  guy hanging out with his favorite nephew  but even the kingpin has a shred of  sympathy in there yes he kills  spider-man with his bare hands and had a  plan that might have destroyed all of  reality but it all comes from his desire  to see his wife and son again their  methods are bad but their desires are  very human and pretty far from world  domination in his first appearance  spider-man didn't start out with his  familiar spider suit instead his first  outing after getting his powers was in a  pretty terrible homemade ensemble and  that's an idea that the movies have kept  around there's always a bad costume  first in spider-man it was a recreation  of a scene from the original comic we  fought a pro wrestler while wearing a  sweat suit and ski mask tricked out with  a stenciled spider on the chest an  amazing spider-man it was a red mask  with dark wraparound shades the MCU  actually flipped the script a little by  starting Peter off at least as far as  the audience is concerned in a comics  accurate version of his famous red and  blue suit provided by Tony Stark it's  only at the end of homecoming that he  goes back to his homemade hoodie into  the spider verse however takes the cake  on this particular trope with bad  costumes all over the place not only  does Peter spend most of the movie  rocking sweatpants and mismatched shoes  but Miles Morales doesn't get his  costume until the very end before that  most of his spidery adventures take  place while he's  wearing a cheap spider-man Halloween  costume purchased of course from smiling  Stanley no refunds incidentally these  are all just bad costumes if you're  looking for the evil costumes well  that's a different guy oh good now  that's vital the heads off order  spider-man's life is pretty much defined  by tragedy it's the core of his  character the hard-luck hero aspect that  makes him so easy to relate to but if  the movies go back to that well all the  time it gets both boring and a little  too depressing as much as we like  watching him get knocked down it's  always better to see him get back up  again that's why the movies always show  us something to remind us that being  spider-man is actually kind of Awesome  and more than a little fun but after  everything I still love being spider-man  I mean who wouldn't be spider-verse  gives us all kinds of moments like this  from spider-man cutting an album of  Christmas carols to the extended comedy  of miles accidentally getting his hand  stuck to Gwen Stacy's hair  throw in some Karate Kid style  web-swinging lessons and it does a great  job of showing us that having  superpowers is actually pretty cool  the Raimi movies do it too leaning into  zany comedy with Peter zipping around  the city on his web lines to deliver  pizza he even looks like he's having fun  with that dance number even if no one  else is amazing spider-man 2 takes that  to the next level with Spidey getting to  his graduation through a series of wild  rides and slapstick crime-fighting high  jinks it's homecoming that does this  best though with the montage of Peters  after-school activities as the friendly  neighborhood superhero not only is he  excited to get out there and start doing  good he gets a churro from a nice lady  that he eats while hanging out on a fire  escape and even does a backflip when  he's asked to by members of a public  that's what sets spider-man apart from  the other superheroes he's the guy who  will stop and argue with you about who  makes the best sandwich sandwich in  Queens  sohave is pretty good too much bread I  like bread check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite superheroes  are coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  you  [Music]
 

